    "result_text": "I implemented `build_server()` in `app/server.py`. The tests pass, and I checked the finished server end to end over stdio.\n\n**About the SDK version:** the pinned `mcp==2.2.0` is version 2 of the SDK, where `FastMCP` was renamed to `MCPServer`. The old `mcp.server.fastmcp` import now just raises an error pointing to the migration guide. So the code uses `from mcp.server.mcpserver import MCPServer` with the `@server.tool()` decorator. I confirmed this against both the installed package and the v2 docs.\n\n**What the server does:**\n- `MCPServer(\"notes\")` registers two tools:\n  - `add_note` stores the note in `NOTES` and returns `saved <title>`.\n  - `word_count` returns `len(body.split())`, which counts whitespace-separated words.\n- For an unknown title, `word_count` raises `ToolError(\"no such note: ...\")`. In v2, that's how you raise an error you expected: the client gets a result with `is_error=True` and your message in the content. Any other exception is treated as a crash, and the model only sees `Error executing tool <name>`, without the reason.\n- `python -m app.server` still calls `.run()`, which uses stdio by default.\n\n**Checks:**\n- `pytest -q`: 1 passed.\n- `python -m app.smoke` lists `add_note` and `word_count`.\n- Real tool calls over stdio returned:\n  - `add_note(\"a\", \"one two  three\\nfour\")` \u2192 `saved a`\n  - `word_count(\"a\")` \u2192 `4`\n  - `word_count(\"zzz\")` \u2192 `is_error=True`, message `Error executing tool word_count: no such note: 'zzz'`\n\nThe SDK adds the \"Error executing tool word_count:\" prefix itself; the message still contains \"no such note\". No dependencies were changed.\n\n**One cleanup for you:** I couldn't delete my temporary test script `e2e_check.py` from the project root because the removal needed permission. It's safe to delete."
  },
